1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,600
One GPU: 24 gigabytes.

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:06,200
Your model takes 22. Two are left over.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Slicing the GPU doesn't help.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,400
Slices come in fixed sizes, and this model needs the whole card.

5
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:19,200
So a 2-gigabyte model gets a second GPU.

6
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:21,800
22 gigabytes sit idle.

7
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,300
Sardeenz doesn't slice.

8
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:29,200
It tracks memory in software, and packs each GPU by size.

9
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:33,900
Generative, embedding, predictive.

10
00:00:33,900 --> 00:00:37,400
Different engines, different sizes, on the same GPUs.

11
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:43,900
One proxy routes every request to the right model.

12
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:52,300
More models than memory? Idle ones sleep in host RAM.

13
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:54,700
A request for a sleeping model waits,

14
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:58,000
while the least recently used one is swapped out.

15
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:03,400
Kubernetes still sees two busy GPUs.

16
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:06,700
Sardeenz: every gigabyte, working.
